selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#pressure-along-the-passage\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Pressure along the passage<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pressure-along-the-passage\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>Aungier\u2019s analysis is lumped: it charges one coefficient per mechanism between\nthe inlet and the collection plane and says nothing about where round the\nvolute each is spent. Spreading them linearly with azimuth is an assumption\nmade so the passage can be plotted and coloured at all, not a result of the\nmodel \u2014 the endpoints are what it actually pins down. The exit cone is on\nfirmer ground, since its losses really are spent between two stations the model\nresolves.</p>", "a[href=\"#constant-area-collectors\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Constant-area collectors<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#constant-area-collectors\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>Swap the area law for a uniform passage \u2014 the unshaped manifold:</p>", "a[href=\"#design-and-solve-a-volute\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Design and solve a volute<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#design-and-solve-a-volute\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>A <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></code> collects a swirling flow around a circumference and delivers it to a\nround pipe. It is the shaped manifold on a pump discharge, and \u2014 through a\ndifferent area law \u2014 the plain constant-area toroidal manifold sometimes used\non a cooling-circuit inlet.</p><p>Three pieces are combined: a <strong>cross-section family</strong>, the <strong>inlet conditions</strong>,\nand an <strong>area law</strong> that decides how big the section is at each azimuth.</p>", "a[href=\"#outlet-ducts\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Outlet ducts<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#outlet-ducts\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>A volute\u2019s full-collection plane connects to a pipe flange whose diameter is\nusually fixed by a piping standard, in a direction that depends on how the\nmachine is packaged: some outlets run straight on, some curve round until they\npoint radially outward, and real hardware sits anywhere between. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ExitCone</span></code>\ndescribes that duct:</p>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute_section/EllipticalSlottedSection.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.EllipticalSlottedSection.inboard_overhang\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.EllipticalSlottedSection.inboard_overhang\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">inboard_overhang</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">size</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">aspect</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>How far the bore reaches inboard of the inlet plane [m].</p><p>Zero for a fully external volute, positive for an intermediate one.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/area_law/VoluteInlet.html#pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.VoluteInlet\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.VoluteInlet\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteInlet</span></span></dt><dd><p>Flow conditions and placement at the volute inlet, Aungier\u2019s station 5.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/exit_cone/ExitCone.html#pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone.ExitCone\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone.ExitCone\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">ExitCone</span></span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/port/Port.html#pyskyfire.ducts.port.Port\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.port.Port\"><code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.port.Port</span></code></a></p><p>How a volute\u2019s outlet duct leaves, and what it grows into.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>Exit-cone geometry reaches the loss analysis three ways.\n<code class=\"xref py py-attr docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">final_diameter</span></code> sets station 7 and, with the arc length, the angle\nthe cone opens out at \u2013 which is most of what decides its diffusion loss.\nThe arc length also sets the wall area friction is charged over, and the\nturn sets the bend loss.</p><p>That makes <code class=\"xref py py-attr docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">arc_length_parameter</span></code> a real design variable rather than\na drawing detail: a longer duct opens out more gradually and recovers more\nof the velocity head, at the cost of more wetted wall and, for a given\nturn, a gentler bend. <code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">loss_breakdown()</span></code>\nreports the three apart so the trade can be seen.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#visualise-it\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Visualise it<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#visualise-it\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute.sweep()</span></code> places each station\u2019s section in machine coordinates, with the\naxis along +z and the tongue at azimuth zero. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteComponent</span></code> wraps that into\na scene. With no exit cone the sweep is capped at both ends, so the step\nbetween the two faces is the cutwater. With one attached, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute.sweep_exit_cone()</span></code>\ncontinues the same tube seamlessly out to the flange \u2013 its first ring is\nbit-identical to the volute\u2019s own last one \u2013 and the cap moves there instead:</p>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.stagnation_pressure\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.stagnation_pressure\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">stagnation_pressure</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Total pressure at each azimuthal station [Pa].</p><p>Aungier\u2019s analysis is lumped: it charges one coefficient per mechanism\nbetween the inlet and the collection plane, and says nothing about\nwhere round the volute each is spent. Spreading them linearly with\nazimuth is an assumption made so the passage can be coloured by\npressure, not a result of the model. It is a defensible one \u2013 the dump\nterms are paid as flow is collected, and collected flow grows linearly\nwith azimuth, while the friction term accumulates along a path length\nthat also grows with azimuth \u2013 but the endpoints are what the model\nactually pins down.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#scope\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Scope<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#scope\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The model is Aungier\u2019s one-dimensional inlet-to-collection-plane analysis. It\ndoes <strong>not</strong> resolve how mass flow distributes around the circumference, so it\ncannot yet tell you whether cooling channels far from the feed point are\nstarved relative to near ones. It also requires incoming swirl: a manifold fed\naxially through a pipe, with no tangential velocity to conserve, is outside\nwhat these correlations describe.</p>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.loss_breakdown\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.loss_breakdown\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">loss_breakdown</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span> <span class=\"sig-return\"><span class=\"sig-return-icon\">\u2192</span> <span class=\"sig-return-typehint\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#dict\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">dict</span></a></span></span></dt><dd><p>Loss coefficients by mechanism, for reporting.</p><p>The three exit terms are disjoint. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_cone</span></code> is the area change\ninto the flange, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_friction</span></code> the wall shear along the duct, and\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_bend</span></code> the cost of turning it. Where the cone tapers either\nway, the first two are the two halves of one Rennels coefficient \u2013\nevaluated at the real friction factor and at zero \u2013 rather than two\ncorrelations that might overlap.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#in-an-engine-network\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">In an engine network<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#in-an-engine-network\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteBlock</span></code> adapts the solver to the network, exactly as <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">PipeBlock</span></code> does:</p>", "a[href=\"#sizing-parameter\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Sizing parameter<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#sizing-parameter\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">SP</span></code> is the ratio of actual passage area to the area that exactly conserves\nangular momentum. It is both the design knob and the parameter the tangential\nloss is computed from, so a designed volute realises the <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">SP</span></code> it was built at.</p><p>The default <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">SP</span> <span class=\"pre\">=</span> <span class=\"pre\">1.0</span></code> zeroes the tangential loss. That is not quite the\nminimum of the <em>total</em> loss: a slightly larger volute slows the flow at the\ncollection plane, which buys back more in friction and exit-cone loss than the\ntangential term costs. Aungier recommends 1.0 to 1.2, biased upward because\nundersizing is penalised far more steeply than oversizing.</p>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/Volute.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">section</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">inlet</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">area_law</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">rotation</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_cone</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">tilt</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">n_stations</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">181</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>A volute or collector resolved from a section family and an area law.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>Geometry is resolved on construction and is independent of the operating\npoint, so one volute can be solved at several mass flows.</p><p>Tilt rotates the section plane rigidly, and rotation mirrors the whole\npassage about a plane through the axis, so both change where the passage\nsits in space but leave every scalar in the loss analysis unchanged. They\nmatter for packaging and for <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">sweep()</span></code></a>, not for pressure loss.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/viz/scene/Scene.html#pyskyfire.viz.scene.Scene.add\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.viz.scene.Scene.add\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">add</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">component</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">name</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">transform</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">position</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">rotation</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">parent</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">anchor</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">attach_to</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">tags</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">()</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/network/blocks/VoluteBlock.html#pyskyfire.network.blocks.VoluteBlock\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.network.blocks.VoluteBlock\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.network.blocks.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteBlock</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">name</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">str</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">st_in</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">str</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">st_out</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">str</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">volute</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/Volute.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">transport</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_diameter</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">|</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/constants.html#None\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">roughness</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">friction_model</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">str</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">'fluids'</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidBlock</span></code></p><p>Engine-network adapter for one <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></code> or collector.</p><p>The contained volute remains independently usable through\n<code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute.solve()</span></code>. This block only translates network stations into that\npublic solver API and emits the resulting outlet station and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">dp_&lt;name&gt;</span></code>\nloss signal. Its geometry is resolved once, when the volute is built, so\nthe network may iterate the operating point freely.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>The volute is adiabatic, so the outlet station carries the inlet\ntemperature. The inlet velocity components are a design input of the volute\nitself rather than something the network sets, so a mass flow that\ndisagrees with them raises\n<a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/VoluteConsistencyWarning.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteConsistencyWarning\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteConsistencyWarning\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteConsistencyWarning</span></code></a> on every solve.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/Volute.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.solve\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.solve\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">solve</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">inlet_state</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/thermo/state/FluidState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidState</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">mass_flow</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">transport</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_diameter</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">|</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/constants.html#None\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">roughness</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">friction_model</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">str</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">'fluids'</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span> <span class=\"sig-return\"><span class=\"sig-return-icon\">\u2192</span> <span class=\"sig-return-typehint\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteResult</span></a></span></span></dt><dd><p>Run Aungier\u2019s meanline loss analysis at one operating point.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#define-the-cross-section\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Define the cross-section<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#define-the-cross-section\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The passage cross-section is an elliptical bore fed through a parallel-sided\nslot, with the wall-to-bore junction rounded on both sides. The slot width is\nfixed by whatever feeds the volute, so the section grows only through its\nbore size:</p>", "a[href=\"#aspect-ratio\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Aspect ratio<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#aspect-ratio\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">aspect_ratio</span></code> is the bore\u2019s semi-axis across the slot over its semi-axis\noutward from the inlet plane. It defaults to one, which is a circle. Above one\nthe bore is stretched across the slot and squashed radially, keeping the volute\nradially compact; below one it reaches further out than it is wide:</p>", "a[href=\"#define-the-inlet-and-build-the-volute\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Define the inlet and build the volute<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#define-the-inlet-and-build-the-volute\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>The inlet carries the radius at which flow is collected and its two velocity\ncomponents. These are design inputs: run the pump sizing first and pass its\noutputs in here.</p>", "a[href=\"#the-tongue\"]": "<h3 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">The tongue<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#the-tongue\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h3><p>A shaped volute wants zero area at the tongue, and no bore that has to span the\nslot can shrink that far. Its <em>shape</em> is not stuck, though, and the aspect\nratio is the way past the floor: holding the size at its minimum and flattening\nthe bore shrinks the area roughly as one over the aspect ratio, without ever\nnarrowing it below the slot. <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></code> does that automatically, up to\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">max_aspect_ratio</span></code>, so the passage tracks the area law considerably further in\nthan a fixed circular bore could:</p>", "a[href=\"#solve-for-pressure-loss\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Solve for pressure loss<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#solve-for-pressure-loss\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>Every loss coefficient is referred to the inlet velocity head, so the total\npressure loss is <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">omega_total</span> <span class=\"pre\">*</span> <span class=\"pre\">rho</span> <span class=\"pre\">*</span> <span class=\"pre\">C_5**2</span> <span class=\"pre\">/</span> <span class=\"pre\">2</span></code>:</p>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/index.html#module-pyskyfire\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>pyskyfire - A Python library for rocket engine simulation and design.</p>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/area_law/ConstantAngularMomentum.html#pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantAngularMomentum\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantAngularMomentum\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">ConstantAngularMomentum</span></span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AreaLaw</span></code></p><p>Size the passage to conserve the angular momentum it collects.</p><p>Imposing <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(r*C_u)_c</span> <span class=\"pre\">=</span> <span class=\"pre\">(r*C_u)_5</span></code> together with mass conservation gives\nAungier\u2019s Eqs. 11-1 to 11-3, generalised by the sizing parameter in\nEq. 11-5:</p><p>so the area divided by the centroid radius grows linearly with azimuth.\nBecause both <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">A</span></code> and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">r_c</span></code> follow from the section size, this is\nimplicit in the size and is solved rather than evaluated.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteResult</span></span></dt><dd><p>Loss breakdown and station conditions for one volute operating point.</p><p>All loss coefficients are referred to the inlet velocity head, so a total\npressure loss follows as <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">omega</span> <span class=\"pre\">*</span> <span class=\"pre\">rho</span> <span class=\"pre\">*</span> <span class=\"pre\">C5**2</span> <span class=\"pre\">/</span> <span class=\"pre\">2</span></code>.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.static_pressure\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.static_pressure\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">static_pressure</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Static pressure at each azimuthal station [Pa].</p><p>The local velocity head taken off the stagnation pressure above, so it\ninherits the same caveat. It starts at the inlet stagnation pressure,\nwhere nothing has been collected and the passage is still.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute_section/EllipticalSlottedSection.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.EllipticalSlottedSection\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.EllipticalSlottedSection\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute_section.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">EllipticalSlottedSection</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">inlet_width</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">fillet_radius</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">throat_length</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">aspect_ratio</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">1.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">max_aspect_ratio</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">4.0</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteSection</span></code></p><p>An elliptical bore opening into a parallel-sided inlet slot.</p><p>This is the cast pump-volute shape: an elliptical passage fed through a\nslot of fixed width <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">inlet_width</span></code>, with the junction between each slot\nwall and the bore rounded by a fillet of user-set radius. At the default\naspect ratio of one the ellipse is a circle, which is the common case.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">size</span></code> is the semi-axis across the slot, so it plays exactly the role the\nbore radius does for a circle and the slot still fixes its floor at\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">inlet_width</span> <span class=\"pre\">/</span> <span class=\"pre\">2</span></code>. Writing <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">c</span></code> for it, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">a</span> <span class=\"pre\">=</span> <span class=\"pre\">c</span> <span class=\"pre\">/</span> <span class=\"pre\">aspect</span></code> for the\nsemi-axis outward from the inlet plane, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">b</span></code> for the inlet width, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">r_f</span></code>\nfor the fillet radius and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">L</span></code> for the throat length, each fillet is\nplaced by requiring it to be tangent to its slot wall and to the bore.</p><p>The fillets round a <em>reflex</em> corner \u2013 the passage opens outward as it\nleaves the slot \u2013 so each fillet centre sits outside the passage at\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">v</span> <span class=\"pre\">=</span> <span class=\"pre\">+/-</span> <span class=\"pre\">(b/2</span> <span class=\"pre\">+</span> <span class=\"pre\">r_f)</span></code> and touches the bore externally. Writing the\ntangency point as the ellipse parameter <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">t</span></code> and the ellipse\u2019s outward\nunit normal there as <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">n(t)</span></code>, the fillet centre is <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">E(t)</span> <span class=\"pre\">+</span> <span class=\"pre\">r_f*n(t)</span></code>,\nand requiring its <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">v</span></code> to equal <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">-(b/2</span> <span class=\"pre\">+</span> <span class=\"pre\">r_f)</span></code> fixes <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">t</span></code> alone:</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/viz/scene/Scene.html#pyskyfire.viz.scene.Scene\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.viz.scene.Scene\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.viz.scene.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">Scene</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">title</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">'Pyskyfire</span> <span class=\"pre\">3D'</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">color_by</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">clim</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">show_axes</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">False</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Assemble components for desktop or standalone HTML display.</p><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">anchor</span></code> names the added component\u2019s local anchor; <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">attach_to</span></code> is a\nworld-space Anchor. Attachment mates their outward x axes in opposition.\nIt is a one-time placement calculation, with no fluid-network constraints.\nExplicit placement and anchor placement are mutually exclusive.</p><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">tags</span></code> labels the added node, a string or an iterable of them. Nodes\nsharing a tag appear together as one group in the HTML viewer, so a\ngrouping written into the script survives regenerating the results.</p><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">show_axes</span></code> adds a corner orientation triad marking the scene axes\n(x red, y green, z blue). It is an orientation cue only, not a measuring\ngrid: it carries no ticks and does not indicate the origin or scale.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/viz/components/VoluteComponent.html#pyskyfire.viz.components.VoluteComponent\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.viz.components.VoluteComponent\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.viz.components.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteComponent</span></span></dt><dd><p>Display a resolved volute or collector passage.</p><p>The volute supplies its own geometry through <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">sweep(n_points=...)</span></code>, which\nplaces each station\u2019s cross-section in machine coordinates, already\ninclined by the volute\u2019s tilt. The local frame is that frame: the machine\naxis is +z, the tongue sits at azimuth zero, and no recentering is applied.</p><p>The passage is swept from the tongue through one revolution, and on through\nits <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">ExitCone</span></code> duct to the flange when it has one.\nThe face at the tongue is a wall \u2013 the cutwater \u2013 so the step visible\nthere is the passage meeting itself.</p><p>Three child nodes come back, so the openings can be told apart from the\nwalls at a glance:</p><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">opening_color</span></code> paints both openings, red by default. They carry no\nfields, so they keep that colour whichever field the scene is coloured by,\nrather than disappearing into the scale.</p><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">results</span></code> is a <code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteResult</span></code>, or a mapping of\nfield names to point-associated FieldData with one value per azimuthal\nstation. A supplied mapping holds its last value along the exit cone, which\na result does only for quantities that have no variation to carry there.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#references\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">References<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#references\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2><p>Aungier, R. H., <em>Centrifugal Compressors: A Strategy for Aerodynamic Design and\nAnalysis</em>, ASME Press, 2000. Sections 5.7 and 11.2.</p><p>Weber, C. R. and Koronowski, M. E., \u201cMeanline Performance Prediction of Volutes\nin Centrifugal Compressors,\u201d ASME 86-GT-216, 1986.</p>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/area_law/ConstantArea.html#pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantArea\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantArea\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">ConstantArea</span></span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AreaLaw</span></code></p><p>Hold the passage area uniform all the way around.</p><p>This is Aungier\u2019s \u201ccollector\u201d rather than a volute. It collects the flow\nwithout conserving angular momentum, so its sizing parameter is whatever\nthe chosen area happens to produce and its tangential-velocity loss is\ncorrespondingly larger. Aungier\u2019s Eq. 5-108 additionally averages that loss\nagainst a total loss of tangential head, because a uniform passage tries to\ndiffuse the entering flow to rest where collection begins.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/viz/scene/Scene.html#pyskyfire.viz.scene.Scene.save_html\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.viz.scene.Scene.save_html\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">save_html</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">path</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd></dd>", "a[href=\"../autoapi/pyskyfire/ducts/volute/VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.exit_cone_pressures\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult.exit_cone_pressures\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_cone_pressures</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">stations</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">9</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Stagnation and static pressure along the exit cone [Pa].</p><p>Unlike the volute passage, the duct\u2019s losses really are spent between\ntwo stations the model pins down, so the stagnation pressure ramps\nlinearly between them rather than apportioning a lumped total. The\nvelocity is eased from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">C_6</span></code> to <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">C_7</span></code> on the same smoothstep the\nswept duct\u2019s section morphs along, so the static pressure follows the\nrendered shape; only its endpoints carry model weight.</p><p>Returns <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(stagnation,</span> <span class=\"pre\">static)</span></code>, each with <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">stations</span></code> values, the\nfirst at the collection plane and the last at the flange.</p></dd>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
